Fixed: stdio MCP servers now start in their own process group, so the first Ctrl+C (which only cancels the current answer) no longer kills them. All checks pass. Committed as a4393b09.

What changed
• One setting, used by both server starts. yoyo starts each MCP server twice: a short pre-check that lists its tools (src/agent_builder.rs) and the real connection (src/mcp_prefix.rs). Both now call the new mcp_stdio_options() helper and yoagent's McpClient::connect_stdio_with. A grep finds 0 calls to the old connect_stdio( left in src/.
• New test, `tests/mcp_process_group.rs` (Unix only): it runs the real binary with a small python3 MCP server that writes down its process id and group from both starts. It checks that neither group is yoyo's and that each server leads its own group. Without python3 it prints SKIPPED and checks nothing. It removes API keys from the environment the same way tests/config_fifo.rs does.
• The test catches the bug: with the setting switched off (in one command, then restored), it failed by name with both servers in yoyo's group, and passed again once restored. No NEUTERED marker is left in the tree.
• Docs: I added a sentence to the mcp_prefix.rs entry in ARCHITECTURE.md (with its version marker) and a paragraph to the Ctrl+C section of docs/src/troubleshooting/common-issues.md.

The cost (posted on #1098)
yoyo never shuts its MCP servers down on exit. It keeps a permanent reference to every connection, and the -p path ends with process::exit, which skips cleanup. So on exit a server only sees its input close. A server that ignores that keeps running after yoyo has gone.
• One run confirmed this: after a yoyo -p was stopped with SIGTERM, the pre-check server was gone, but a test server built to ignore closed input was still running.
• The REPL exits (/quit, Ctrl+D, double Ctrl+C) and closing the terminal I worked out from the code without running them. Closing the terminal is the one case that changes: the hangup used to reach the server and now doesn't.

That leftover is filed as #1099: shut each server down on exit, with a time limit. I filed it separately because "Fixes #1098" closes #1098 when this is pushed.
